 ],
 "notes": "48 stations, nos. 115-162, in six sub-headings: Isle of Man (printed \"Isle of Man.\" with a full stop), N. W. England, North Wales, South Wales (printed with no full stop), Bristol Channel and South-Western England. 29 rows have an asterisk (returns made).\n\nHow I recorded things:\n- Fragments: I removed the indentation at the start of each line and kept all spacing inside the line. The two wrapped keeper lines (4659 \"and Hughes.\", 4662 \"Hughes.\") are kept as separate fragments.\n- Numbers: each is printed with a full stop (\"115.\"), which I left off in the number field.\n- Names: kept with the comma or full stop that ends them in print (\"Langness,\", \"Point of Ayre.\").\n- Keepers: I left off the full stop that ends each row (\"Robt. Clyne.\" became \"Robt. Clyne\").\n- Messrs entries: kept as one entry each, as you asked: \"Messrs Work and Clague\" (Dee), \"Messrs Pritchard, Owen, and Hughes\" (Holyhead Breakwater, joined across lines 4658-4659), \"Messrs Thomas and Hughes\" (Carnarvon Bay, joined across 4661-4662). Each one names several keepers.\n\nPoints a reader should know:\n- 148 Bideford Bar has a keeper named (Thomas Gilpin) but no asterisk. It is the only row like this, so it may have sent a schedule but no return, or the asterisk may have been dropped.\n- 124 is printed just \"Air.\" This is presumably Point of Air (Point of Ayr, Flintshire), not to be confused with 115 Point of Ayre on the Isle of Man.\n- Spellings kept as printed: \"Chickens Rock\" (no apostrophe), \"Caldy\" (Caldey), \"Flatholm\", \"Sevenstones\", \"Carnarvon Bay\", \"St Tudwal's\", \"Bishop's Rock\", \"Nash (E.)\" (East Nash).\n- Types: 121 Selker and 154 Sevenstones are printed L.V. (light-vessel). Every row has its type printed, and none of them looked wrong to me.\n- The list has no light characters and nothing else printed in any row, so character and other are null throughout. The places are printed only as sub-headings, so place is null and they appear under coast.\n- Lines 4706-4715, just after the list, name Henry Nicholas, Robert Clyne (spelled out in full there, \"Robt.\" in the list) and Messrs Work and Clague as having sent wings of birds. That may be useful for the roll of keepers.",
